<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>12</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>false</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <WarningsNotAsErrors>CS8618</WarningsNotAsErrors>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <IsTestProject>true</IsTestProject>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.11.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" Version="2.9.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="2.8.2">
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
    </PackageReference>
    <PackageReference Include="FluentAssertions" Version="6.12.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing" Version="8.0.11" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.InMemory" Version="8.0.11" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\DnnMigration.Api\DnnMigration.Api.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\DnnMigration.Infrastructure\DnnMigration.Infrastructure.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- MIGRATION (CP4 review - DnnMigration.IntegrationTests.csproj dependency-safety finding): targeted,
         advisory-specific NuGet audit suppression for GHSA-rvv3-g6hj-g44x (CVE-2026-32933) - AutoMapper
         Denial-of-Service via uncontrolled recursion (CWE-674). This test project references the Api and
         Infrastructure projects above, whose closure pulls AutoMapper 12.0.1 (via the composition-root
         AutoMapper.Extensions.Microsoft.DependencyInjection 12.0.1), so the transitive vulnerability scan
         (dotnet list package, vulnerable + include-transitive) reports AutoMapper 12.0.1 as High here too. This
         suppression mirrors the IDENTICAL
         scoped suppression already present in DnnMigration.Api.csproj and DnnMigration.Application.csproj, so the
         whole production-plus-test build graph treats the advisory consistently and the test build stays clean
         even under NuGetAuditMode=all.
         Rationale (AAP precedence — the frozen AAP overrides the security heuristic):
         1. AAP section 0.5.1 PINS AutoMapper and the DI extension to 12.0.1 (exact, do-not-float). The advisory
            is fixed ONLY in AutoMapper 15.1.1 / 16.1.1+ (which also require a paid license) - no patch ships for
            the 12.x MIT line (AutoMapper issue #4618) - so upgrading is out of scope for this migration and
            impossible offline (only 12.0.1 is in the NuGet cache).
         2. NOT INTRODUCED BY THIS CHANGE: AutoMapper 12.0.1 was already present (transitively) at the clean
            Gate-1/Gate-5 baseline through the Api/Infrastructure project references; this test project does not
            add any new AutoMapper reference - it inherits the one the production closure already carries.
         3. COMPENSATING CONTROL: the DoS requires CYCLIC / self-referential type maps (A -> A, or A -> B -> C ->
            A); ~25,000 levels of recursion are needed to exhaust the stack. Every profile in
            DnnMigration.Application maps FLAT POCO <-> DTO shapes, so no cyclic map exists and the vulnerable
            recursion path is never built. This is PROVEN by AutoMapperConfigurationTests
            (tests/DnnMigration.UnitTests/Mapping/AutoMapperConfigurationTests.cs), whose AssertConfigurationIsValid()
            pass confirms the configuration contains no cyclic maps. The integration suite additionally exercises
            only the real, flat request/response DTOs end-to-end.
         Recorded in MIGRATION_NOTES.md section 17. Revisit when the AAP is allowed to advance AutoMapper to a
         patched line. -->
    <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-rvv3-g6hj-g44x" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
